 "title": "Живой гусь  (The Live Goose)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "barinov": "Шеф",
  "max": "Макс"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "This is The Kitchen, the Russian comedy about a hot-tempered chef and the staff of a smart Moscow restaurant called Claude Monet. In this third episode, Max, the young cook, has spent a week unloading deliveries and is longing to be allowed near a stove. Instead, Chef Barinov hands him something very different: a live goose. Here is their conversation in Russian first, so don't worry about catching every word.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Доброе утро, шеф!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Здравствуй, огузок. Вот твоя работа.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, это живой гусь!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "У нас сегодня очень важный банкет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Зачем нам живой гусь на кухне?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Гостям нужен самый свежий продукт!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Но я повар, а не мясник.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Каждый повар обязан уметь разделывать мясо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Что, мне нужно убить его?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А ты думал, мясо растёт на деревьях?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я никогда в жизни этого не делал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Убей гуся, или ты уволен!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Хорошо, я попробую сделать это.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's go back and watch Max's face.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Доброе утро, шеф!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning, chef!' Here we see ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Доброе утро,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'good morning', and it puts a special ending on 'good' because the word for morning, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "утро,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " uses a middle shape.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Здравствуй, огузок. Вот твоя работа.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Hello, oguzok. Here is your work.' Looking closely, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Здравствуй,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to the hello for one person you know well. Add an extra sound at the end and you get the polite version. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "огузок.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for the rump cut in a cow, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Вот твоя работа.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means 'here your work'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, это живой гусь!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, this is a live goose!' Looking closely, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "это живой гусь!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally says 'this live goose', with no action word for 'is'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "живой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'alive', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "гусь!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for a goose.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "У нас сегодня очень важный банкет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We have a very important banquet today.' Pay attention to ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "У нас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means 'at us', which is how they say 'we have'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "важный",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'important', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "банкет.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for the banquet.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Зачем нам живой гусь на кухне?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why do we need a live goose in the kitchen?' Focusing here, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Зачем нам",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'what for, to us', and that second word carries the feeling of 'we need'. Note ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "на кухне?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " — they literally say they are 'on' the kitchen, not 'in' it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Гостям нужен самый свежий продукт!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The guests need the freshest product!' The word for the guests, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Гостям",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", gets a 'to' ending, because the thing needed is the main focus. So the needed word matches with ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "продукт!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", which has a guy shape, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "самый свежий",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is built from 'most fresh'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Но я повар, а не мясник.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'But I am a cook, not a butcher.' In ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "я повар, а не мясник.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", the little phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "а не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'and not', setting one job against the other. The word for butcher, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "мясник.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", is built from the word for meat.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Каждый повар обязан уметь разделывать мясо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Every chef is obliged to know how to butcher meat.' Looking closely, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "обязан",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'obliged', and has a man's ending. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "уметь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for 'know how' when talking about skills, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "разделывать",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means to cut an animal into pieces.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Что, мне нужно убить его?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What, do I need to kill it?' Observe how ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "мне нужно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally says 'to me necessary', which is the standard way to say 'I need to'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "убить",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the word for 'to kill', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "его?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'him', refers to the goose.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А ты думал, мясо растёт на деревьях?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Did you think meat grows on trees?' Listen carefully, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "думал,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " ends in a specific sound because Barinov is talking to a man. The word for tree changes into a strange shape for more than one, making 'on trees' look like ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "на деревьях?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я никогда в жизни этого не делал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I have never done this in my life.' In Russian, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "никогда",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "не делал.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally pair up to say 'never not did', which is their way of saying 'not' twice. The word for 'this', ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "этого",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", takes an 'of' shape because of the negative, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "в жизни",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'in life'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Убей гуся, или ты уволен!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Kill the goose, or you are fired!' Let us review ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Убей",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to the action word 'kill!'. The word for the goose becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "гуся,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " because it is the living creature receiving the action. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "ты уволен!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means 'you are fired'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Хорошо, я попробую сделать это.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Fine, I will try to do it.' Focusing here, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "попробую",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'I will try', which is the finished-action partner shape of the base action word. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "сделать это.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is built from the words for 'to do it'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Доброе утро, шеф!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Здравствуй, огузок. Вот твоя работа.",
   "gap": 0.5
